 "literal": "Digging the earth they will not enter, the sky they will not climb / The wide, dark ocean by foot they will not cross / Country by country, village by village / House by house, if we search / Are there those who can remain lost, our lovers?",
 "translation": "They cannot dig into the earth and hide, nor can they climb up into the sky. They cannot walk across the wide, dark ocean. If we search country by country, village by village, house by house, can our lover really remain lost to us?",
 "modern": "அவர்கள் பூமியைத் தோண்டி உள்ளே நுழைய மாட்டார்கள்; வானத்தில் ஏறிப் போக மாட்டார்கள்; குறுக்கே இருக்கும் கரிய கடலைக் காலால் நடந்து கடக்க மாட்டார்கள். நாட நாடாக, ஊர் ஊராக, ஒவ்வொரு குடிசையாகத் தேடிப் பார்த்தால், நம் காதலர் நம்மிடமிருந்து தப்பித்து மறைந்து வாழ முடியுமா? முடியாது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine's companion to console the heroine, who is deeply distressed by her lover's long absence. The companion reassures her that the lover cannot have vanished completely from the world. She argues that he cannot escape by digging into the earth, ascending to the heavens, or walking across the vast ocean. By systematically searching every country, village, and household, they are bound to find him. This reassurance belongs to the Pālai landscape, where themes of separation and the anxiety of waiting are addressed with hope and determination.",
